 ],
 "answer_text": "Found 3 gene records for Li-Fraumeni syndrome: cyclin dependent kinase inhibitor 2A [1], checkpoint kinase 2 [4] and tumor protein p53 [5], linked to 1 disease: Li-Fraumeni syndrome [2]. \n\nThree genes are associated with Li-Fraumeni syndrome: NCBIGene:1029, named cyclin dependent kinase inhibitor 2A [1]. The disease record for this association is Li-Fraumeni syndrome [2]. \n\nLi-Fraumeni syndrome is a cancer predisposition syndrome caused by a germline mutation of the TP53 gene on chromosome 17p13.1, inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern with high penetrance [3]. Component tumors include soft tissue sarcomas, osteosarcoma, premenopausal breast cancer, brain tumors, and adrenal cortical carcinomas [3]. \n\nDisease records found\n\nDisease name: Li-Fraumeni syndrome [2]. \n\nGene-to-disease mapping\n\nGene NCBIGene:1029, name: cyclin dependent kinase inhibitor 2A [1]. Gene NCBIGene:11200, name: checkpoint kinase 2 [4]. Gene NCBIGene:7157, name: tumor protein p53 [5]. \n\nPubmed records found\n\nPubmed title: Li-Fraumeni Syndrome [6]. Pubmed title: Li-Fraumeni syndrome [10]. Pubmed title: Li-Fraumeni Syndrome [12]. Pubmed title: Li-Fraumeni syndrome heterogeneity [14]. \n\nMedgen records found\n\nMedgen title: Li-Fraumeni syndrome [7]. \n\nLiterature entity records found\n\nLiterature entity name: Li-Fraumeni Syndrome [8]. \n\nClinical trial records found\n\nClinical trial name: Clinical, Genetic, Behavioral, Laboratory and Epidemiologic Characterization of Individuals and Families at High Risk of Breast/Ovarian Cancer [9]. Clinical trial name: Role of p53 Gene in Metabolism Regulation in Patients With Li-Fraumeni Syndrome [11]. Clinical trial name: Characterization of Patients With Uncommon Presentations and/or Uncommon Diseases Associated With the Cardiovascular System [13]. Clinical trial name: Clinical and Genetic Studies of Li-Fraumeni Syndrome [15]. Clinical trial name: LIFSCREEN : Evaluation of Whole Body MRI for Early Detection of Cancers in Subjects With P53 Mutation (Li-Fraumeni Syndrome) [16]. "
